<BaseLayout title="About Freighthub" description="Who writes Freighthub, what it covers, how it stays independent, and what it will not do.">
  <article>
    <h1>About Freighthub</h1>
    <p class="article-meta">Independent editorial analysis · Last reviewed 11 November 2026</p>

    <h2>What this site is</h2>
    <p>Freighthub is an independent editorial project about moving goods from China to the six GCC markets: Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates, Qatar, Kuwait, Oman and Bahrain. It covers ports, routes, customs, certification, sectors and the commercial decisions that sit between them.</p>
    <p>It is not a freight forwarder, a carrier, a customs broker or a marketplace. It does not sell freight, take bookings or earn commissions on shipments. That independence is the point: the analysis is not shaped by a carrier's margin or a forwarder's network.</p>

    <h2>Who writes it</h2>
    <p>Freighthub is written and maintained by the <strong>Freighthub editorial desk</strong>. The desk sets the questions, checks the sources, edits the copy and owns the judgements. Research and drafting are supported by AI tools under editorial direction; every published page is reviewed against the standards in our <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> before it goes live.</p>
    <p>We do not publish a fictional author biography or invented credentials. If a page says "Freighthub editorial", that is the byline: a desk, not a person pretending to be one.</p>

    <h2>What we cover</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Countries</strong> — the six GCC markets, their import rules and their commercial logic.</li>
      <li><strong>Ports</strong> — the gateways that matter for China-origin cargo, from Jebel Ali to Salalah.</li>
      <li><strong>Guides</strong> — the methods: FCL, LCL, air, DDP, customs clearance, certification, cost and transit.</li>
      <li><strong>Sectors</strong> — electronics, batteries, chemicals, food, furniture, machinery and ecommerce.</li>
      <li><strong>Compare</strong> — the decisions: which port, which mode, which market, who clears.</li>
      <li><strong>Updates</strong> — dated briefings on conditions that change the calculation.</li>
      <li><strong>Notes</strong> — original arguments about how the market actually works.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What we will not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>We will not quote a precise freight rate.</strong> Rates move weekly and depend on the shipment. We give planning ranges and the drivers behind them.</li>
      <li><strong>We will not guarantee a transit time.</strong> We give ranges and explain what moves them.</li>
      <li><strong>We will not name clients or claim certifications we do not have.</strong> Case material is anonymised or omitted.</li>
      <li><strong>We will not pretend a scaffold is a finished page.</strong> Pages without substantive content are marked noindex and kept out of the sitemap.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How the analysis is built</h2>
    <p>Each page starts with the operational question a shipper would actually ask: how long, how much, what can go wrong, who is responsible. The research then separates three kinds of statement:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Reported</strong> — a fact from an official source or the trade press, cited.</li>
      <li><strong>Industry claim</strong> — a range or practice attributed to forwarders, carriers or importers, labelled as such.</li>
      <li><strong>Editorial judgement</strong> — our assessment, labelled and arguable.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The labels matter. A site that mixes all three into one confident voice is easier to write and harder to trust.</p>

    <h2>Independence and funding</h2>
    <p>Freighthub is self-funded. It does not run display advertising, sponsored posts or affiliate links to forwarders. If that changes, the change will be disclosed on this page and on the relevant pages, not buried in a footer.</p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>We make mistakes. When we do, we fix them and log the fix in <a href="/corrections/">corrections</a>. If you find an error, the contact details are on the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>

    <h2>How to use the site</h2>
    <p>Start with the market you care about: <a href="/guides/china-to-saudi-arabia/">Saudi Arabia</a>, the <a href="/guides/china-to-uae/">UAE</a>, <a href="/guides/china-to-qatar/">Qatar</a>, <a href="/guides/china-to-kuwait/">Kuwait</a>, <a href="/guides/china-to-oman/">Oman</a> or <a href="/guides/china-to-bahrain/">Bahrain</a>. Then the method: <a href="/guides/customs-clearance/">customs clearance</a>, <a href="/guides/certification/">certification</a>, <a href="/guides/ddp/">DDP</a> and <a href="/guides/cost-and-transit/">cost and transit</a>.</p>

    <h2>A note on scope</h2>
    <p>The site covers China-origin cargo into the GCC. It does not cover Europe, the Americas, Africa or the broader Middle East beyond the GCC. That is a deliberate limit: a smaller scope with deeper content is more useful than a broad directory with shallow pages.</p>
    <p>It also does not cover every port or every product. The ports are the ones that matter for China–GCC container, project and bulk cargo; the sectors are the ones with distinct compliance or handling requirements. If a page is missing, it is because the information is not yet good enough to publish, not because the topic is unimportant.</p>
